WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.240
- So, the Senate failed
to procure enough votes

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.880
to convict Former President Trump

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.460
of the charge of
incitement to insurrection

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.300
and thus, in a way, the Senate
has acquitted President Trump

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.730
of his record second impeachment.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.290
For many, this was a fait accompli,

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.260
that there was simply
no way it was possible

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.840
for the Democrats or anyone else

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.460
to convince enough Republicans

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.980
to form the necessary 2/3 majority

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.870
in order to convict on impeachment.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:25.510
But even still this
was a marked difference

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:26.780
from the first impeachment trial

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.950
and a record number of members

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.650
of the same party as the former president

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:32.990
voted to convict.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:34.120
But let's take a look at the five days

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.250
that comprise the impeachment trial,

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.700
examine the legal arguments,

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.370
and see if this was indeed inevitable

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.700
and whether the arguments

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.970
on the prosecution side
and the defense side

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.197
held any water.

00:00:44.197 --> 00:00:46.780
(bright music)

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.280
Hey, LegalEagles, it's time to think

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.280
like an impeachment lawyer,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.470
because Former President Trump

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:54.070
was able to escape
conviction on impeachment

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.990
for a record second time,

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:57.770
but this time only narrowly.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:00.080
Now, as we've talked about
many times on this channel,

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.000
an impeachment trial is
not a criminal trial.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.010
You know this.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.690
You know that it is a political trial,

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.200
or more specifically it's
a trial by Parliament.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.890
And lawyers like myself
like to make analogies

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.180
to the criminal justice system,

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.350
but these analogies are always imperfect,

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.330
and this moves in several
different directions.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.610
So we're gonna be talking
about things like due process

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.430
and the hearsay rule,

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.740
but often these things
are completely inchoate

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.450
in an impeachment setting

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:26.990
because the Senate makes its own rules.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.300
It can adopt to the
federal rules of evidence

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.710
but it doesn't have to,

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.350
and due process considerations

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.010
that exist for private citizens

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.360
in the criminal justice system

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.490
simply do not exist in
an impeachment setting.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.610
But that doesn't necessarily mean

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:40.920
that we need to throw any of that out,

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.160
it just means that it might
not necessarily carry the day.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.760
And we saw this throughout
the impeachment trial

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.530
with lawyers making
contradictory arguments,

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.980
with senators justifying their reasons

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.310
for voting or not voting for
conviction of impeachment

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.610
in ways that absolutely do not jive

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.380
with the criminal justice system.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.590
And we also saw things
like a group of senators

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.450
meeting with the defense lawyers.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.770
We saw Senators Cruz, Graham, and Lee

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.840
actually meet with
President Trump's surrogates

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.120
to discuss the defense of the President.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.840
And people were absolutely
right to point out

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.900
that in a criminal justice setting

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.400
members of the jury
meeting and strategizing

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.790
with attorneys for either side

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.670
would be completely improper.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.430
This is not a criminal justice setting.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.730
Literally the impeachment
clause of the Constitution

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.530
does not allow for criminal penalties.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.990
It's on a narrow issue
of preventing someone

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.130
from holding office again.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:34.560
So, look, politicians are
going to be politicians.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.070
But that being said, let's
dive into the legal arguments

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.000
that were made.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.340
So let's start with the very first

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.100
foundational jurisdictional question,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.810
whether the Senate can even
hold an impeachment hearing

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.270
related to an official who is
no longer in that position.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.710
The first day of the impeachment trial

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.560
was devoted to this very question,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.390
because generally you have
to determine issues of law

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.750
before you can get to the issues of fact.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.050
Some argued that the Constitution

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.160
doesn't answer this question directly,

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.430
so let's start with the historical context

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:03.790
that the Framers were dealing with.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.227
- 'Cause as Hamilton wrote,

00:03:05.227 --> 00:03:07.137
"England provided the model

00:03:07.137 --> 00:03:10.530
"from which the idea of this
institution has been borrowed."

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.170
- In fact, every impeachment that occurred

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.440
during the Framers' lifetime

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.000
actually concerned a former official.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.340
And the consensus of the first states

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.740
to draft their own constitutions

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.260
was that impeachment of former
officials was not banned.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.080
- Every single state
constitution in the 1780s

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.100
either specifically said
that former officials

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.690
could be impeached

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:32.150
or were entirely consistent with the idea.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.300
In contrast, not a
single state constitution

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.780
prohibited trials of former officials.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.920
- And Virginia, Delaware,
Vermont, and Pennsylvania

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.640
explicitly permitted late impeachment

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:44.210
in their state constitutions,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.270
which were written before
the US Constitution.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.790
And, generally speaking, in
the pre-constitutional era,

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.390
officers were impeached
after they left office.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.390
In Vermont, for example,

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:53.950
State Representative Jonathan Fassett

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.310
was impeached after he joined an angry mob

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.830
that wanted to stop a
county court from convening.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.830
Huh, I wonder if that sounds familiar.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.817
He was impeached and later
convicted after he left office.

00:04:03.817 --> 00:04:05.580
And the Constitution was being hashed out

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.480
during the same month

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.250
that British Governor-General
of India William Hastings

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.000
was being impeached for corruption.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.700
Hastings was already out
of office at the time.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:14.990
The people who drafted the Constitution

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.100
repeatedly referred to
the Hastings impeachment

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.460
in an offhand manner,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:19.880
the same way that people in our era

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:21.500
may refer to the Clinton impeachment

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.360
without getting into the gritty details.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.900
And apparently the Founders did not object

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.600
to the lateness of this impeachment.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.010
Instead, they used it to show
why impeachment was important.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.960
George Mason noted that Hastings
committed a slew of crimes

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.110
but none of them fit the
definition of treason.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.130
Mason warned that the
definition of treason

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.460
in the US Constitution

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.310
would not include attempts to
"subvert the Constitution."

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.200
Therefore, impeachment should include

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.920
high crimes and misdemeanors.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.900
And George Mason certainly imagined

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.510
someone like Donald Trump.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:49.343
In other words,

00:04:49.343 --> 00:04:51.040
Mason thought that making
a character judgment

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.110
was an important part of the
scheme of checks and balances

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:55.760
between the branches of government.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.760
So, Lin-Manuel, where is
George Mason's musical?

00:04:58.760 --> 00:04:59.593
So, not surprisingly,

00:04:59.593 --> 00:05:01.350
almost the entire first day
of the impeachment trial

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.380
was devoted to this legal question.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:05.910
Can the Senate hold an impeachment trial

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.060
related to an official
who has either resigned

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.460
or whose term has expired?

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.570
And generally that's a good idea

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:13.710
because if this was a criminal trial

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.360
you would almost always dispose
of all the legal questions

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.550
before getting to the issues of fact.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.190
And that's especially important here

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.740
because this is a political question

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.810
where the Supreme Court
would never touch it

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:25.230
with a 10-foot pole.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.063
In other words,

00:05:26.063 --> 00:05:28.720
even if Former President
Trump was convicted,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.670
there's no way that he
could try to appeal this

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:31.503
to the federal courts

00:05:31.503 --> 00:05:34.410
and say that this was
improper or unconstitutional.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.160
The Supreme Court would
100% never answer it

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.620
because this is a political question

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.630
that's left up to the judicial branch.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:45.270
And, so, given that on the
first day the Senate held a vote

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.760
and held by a majority
that the Senate believed

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.930
it had jurisdiction to hold
this impeachment trial,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.570
one would think that this
legal question was settled.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:57.200
But of course this is
partially a political question

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.860
and thus the senators
can do whatever they want

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.840
for basically whatever reasons they want.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.320
But there is I think one important analogy

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:05.830
to the criminal justice system

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.020
that applies in a situation like this,

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.300
because the way that a
criminal trial would work

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.760
is that the lawyers and the judge

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.620
would hash out all of the legal issues

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.010
and eventually, once those
things are hashed out,

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.960
the judge will give the jury
instructions to the jury

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.900
that gives them all of
the law that they need

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.800
to understand the decision
that they're going to make.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.640
And there's a phrase that we
use to describe what happens

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.410
when a jury disregards
those kind of instructions

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.810
and makes a decision based on
whatever rational they want,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.310
and that phrase is jury nullification.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.120
And it's completely reasonable to say

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:39.570
that several members of the Senate

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.250
engaged in jury nullification

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.170
to find Former President Trump not guilty.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:46.820
And then there's also the
doctrine of unclean hands

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:49.580
because it's not like
the date of this trial

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:52.230
was set in stone from on high.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:54.320
Let's not forget that former
Senate majority leader

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.070
Mitch McConnell was in control
of the Senate's schedule

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:59.910
and prevented the Senate
from holding a trial

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:03.740
on Former President Trump
while he was still in office.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.650
And that's particularly galling

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:08.140
when Mitch McConnell used that timing

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.950
as the reason that he
didn't vote to convict,

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:15.060
saying that impeachment is
primarily a tool for removal,

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.850
though he leaves open the argument

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.140
that impeachment can be used
outside of that context,

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:23.900
which the majority of legal
scholars believe as well.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:24.780
And some have argued,

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.830
again analogizing to the
criminal justice system,

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.610
that if you in fact believe

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.790
that the Senate doesn't have jurisdiction,

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:34.750
then you can't participate in that vote.

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:35.860
But there is one senator

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:37.380
who I think threaded this needle

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.750
and actually came to a principled stance

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.570
while fundamentally disagreeing

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:42.980
with some of these
jurisdictional questions,

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.680
and that's Senator Richard
Burr from North Carolina

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:47.610
who recognized that he couldn't hide

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.970
behind the unconstitutionality argument

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:51.470
and voted to convict.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.287
He stated, "When this process started,

00:07:53.287 --> 00:07:54.797
"I believed that it was unconstitutional

00:07:54.797 --> 00:07:57.307
"to impeach a president who
was no longer in office.

00:07:57.307 --> 00:07:59.217
"I still believe that to be the case.

00:07:59.217 --> 00:08:01.547
"However, the Senate is an
institution based on precedent,

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:03.247
"and given that the majority of the Senate

00:08:03.247 --> 00:08:04.917
"voted to proceed with this trial,

00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:06.287
"the question of constitutionality

00:08:06.287 --> 00:08:08.007
"is now established precedent.

00:08:08.007 --> 00:08:09.187
"As an impartial juror,

00:08:09.187 --> 00:08:10.517
"my role is now to determine

00:08:10.517 --> 00:08:12.477
"whether House managers have
sufficiently made the case

00:08:12.477 --> 00:08:14.927
"for the article of impeachment
against President Trump.

00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:16.977
"I've listened to the arguments
presented by both sides

00:08:16.977 --> 00:08:18.277
"and considered the facts.

00:08:18.277 --> 00:08:19.920
"The facts are clear."

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.540
And then voted for conviction.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.450
Those are the words of someone
who had a reasonable argument

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:27.210
related to the process
of the impeachment trial

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.850
but then did his duty
based on his position

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.970
as the impeachment went forward.

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:33.550
So, with that historical context,

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:36.730
let's look at the actual
text of the Constitution.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:38.023
Article II, Section 4 says,

00:08:38.023 --> 00:08:39.477
"The president, vice president,

00:08:39.477 --> 00:08:41.917
"and all civil officers
of the United States

00:08:41.917 --> 00:08:43.957
"shall be removed from
office on impeachment

00:08:43.957 --> 00:08:45.997
"for and conviction of treason, bribery,

00:08:45.997 --> 00:08:48.600
"or other high crimes and misdemeanors."

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.220
Trump argues that the plain
meaning of the word officer

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:53.200
means a person who currently holds office,

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.850
not an ex-officer.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:57.060
The phrase "removed from
office" in this context

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.070
would mean that once a
person was already gone,

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:00.980
he or she cannot be removed.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.810
And Trump lawyer David Schoen
addressed this argument

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:04.643
in his trial brief

00:09:04.643 --> 00:09:06.470
but didn't discuss it in any detail

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:07.740
during his opening statement.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.180
The Democrats argued that the question

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:10.890
of whether a former official can be tried

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.410
as part of the impeachment process

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:14.940
depends not just on the language
of Article II, Section 4,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.110
but also depends on other
sections of the Constitution

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.530
that deal with impeachment.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:19.700
They begin with the question,

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:21.930
how does the Constitution
describe the accused

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:23.640
in an impeachment trial?

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.660
We already know that Article
II, Section 4 simply says

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.940
the president, vice president,
and all civil officers

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:29.240
can be removed.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:31.240
Trump's lawyers rely
exclusively on this provision

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.270
to make their argument
that only current officers

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:34.460
can be impeached.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.500
And House managers rely on Chuck Cooper,

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:37.820
a staunchly conservative lawyer

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.060
who has represented Republicans

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:40.790
including Minority Leader Mark Meadows.

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:41.800
- As Cooper says,

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:44.490
Article II, Section 4
means just what it says.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.220
The first half describes

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.400
what an official must do to be impeached,

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.360
namely commit high
crimes and misdemeanors,

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.210
and the second half describes

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:55.830
what happens when civil
officers of the United States,

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.070
including the sitting president,

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.360
are convicted.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:09:59.940
Removal from office.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.550
That's it.
- In Cooper's words,

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:03.670
that establishes what is
known in the criminal law

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.090
as a mandatory minimum punishment.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:07.540
And then we move on to the next section.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.440
To define the identity of the accused

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:10.390
in an impeachment trial,

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:11.830
the House managers also rely

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:14.270
on Article I, Section 3, Clause 6 and 7,

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:16.340
which refer to a person and a party

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.040
rather than a president, vice
president, or civil officer.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.087
Clause 6, "The Senate
shall have the sole power

00:10:22.087 --> 00:10:23.467
"to try all impeachments.

00:10:23.467 --> 00:10:24.447
"When sitting for that purpose,

00:10:24.447 --> 00:10:26.437
"they shall be on oath or affirmation.

00:10:26.437 --> 00:10:28.077
"When the President of the
United States is tried,

00:10:28.077 --> 00:10:29.777
"the Chief Justice shall preside.

00:10:29.777 --> 00:10:31.637
"And no person shall be convicted

00:10:31.637 --> 00:10:34.940
"without the concurrence of
2/3 of the members present."

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:36.787
Then we move on to Clause 7.

00:10:36.787 --> 00:10:38.427
"Judgment in cases of impeachment

00:10:38.427 --> 00:10:40.917
"shall not extend further
than removal from office

00:10:40.917 --> 00:10:43.507
"and the disqualification
to hold enjoyment

00:10:43.507 --> 00:10:45.447
"of any office of honor, trust, or profit

00:10:45.447 --> 00:10:46.617
"under the United States.

00:10:46.617 --> 00:10:49.417
"But the party convicted
shall nevertheless be liable

00:10:49.417 --> 00:10:51.817
"and subject to indictment,
trial, and punishment

00:10:51.817 --> 00:10:53.460
"according to law."

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:55.110
So here we have the words person and party

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.350
rather than a civil officer

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:58.150
in describing the accused.

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:00.990
Arguably, this word choice
includes former officials.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:03.730
- Who could be put on trial,
rather, for impeachment

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:04.840
other than civil officers?

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:08.040
Who else could a person or a party be?

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:10.460
Well, really, there's
only one possible answer.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:11.760
Former officers.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:12.700
- And then circling back

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:14.040
to some prior language that we read,

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:15.810
the House managers also
direct our attention

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.160
back to the language of
Article I, Section 3,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.140
which states judgment
in cases of impeachment

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:21.260
shall not extend further

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.080
than to removal from office
and disqualification.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.220
Representative Joe
Neguse gave a masterclass

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.270
in the construction of these words.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:28.687
- [Joe] The Constitution does not say,

00:11:28.687 --> 00:11:32.760
"Removal from office and
then disqualification."

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:33.593
It doesn't say,

00:11:33.593 --> 00:11:36.820
"Removal from office followed
by disqualification."

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:37.653
- Now, on the other hand,

00:11:37.653 --> 00:11:39.410
the Trump argument is that the and binds

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:41.790
both the removal and
disqualification together

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.300
and thus you can't have a removal

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.560
of someone who's already
been removed from office,

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.562
meaning that therefore you
can't have the disqualification

00:11:49.562 --> 00:11:52.380
against someone who's already
been removed from office.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.500
The vast majority of legal scholars

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:57.120
do not adhere to this
particular interpretation

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.880
and are following what
Representative Neguse

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.080
was already arguing.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.200
But then we go back to
Article I, Section 3,

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:05.057
which states that "the Senate
shall have the sole power

00:12:05.057 --> 00:12:06.920
"to try all impeachments."

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.090
The House managers argue
that all means all.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:10.680
It doesn't say the Senate has the power

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.360
to try impeachments
against sitting officers.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.040
What we're left with is
an impeachment mechanism

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.130
that includes four possible punishments.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:18.400
Impeachment by the House,

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:20.300
removal from office by the Senate,

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:22.330
disqualification from
holding office again,

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:24.830
and a criminal prosecution
in a court of law.

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:26.960
And, of course, many have
argued the policy argument

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.680
that a disqualification was
subject to a time limit.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.470
That creates a dangerous loophole,

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:33.540
a potential January exception.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.930
- Officials could commit
the most extraordinary

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:38.630
destructive offenses
against the American people,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.230
high crimes and misdemeanors,

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.340
and they'd have total control

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.300
over whether they can ever be impeached,

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.210
and, if they are,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.830
whether the Senate can try the case.

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:50.730
If they want to escape any public inquiry

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.180
into their misconduct

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:54.763
or the risk of disqualification
from future office,

00:12:54.763 --> 00:12:55.760
then it's pretty simple.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.100
They could just resign

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:59.360
one minute before the House impeaches,

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:01.120
or even one minute
before the Senate trial,

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:03.350
or they could resign
during the Senate trial

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:04.750
if it's not looking so well.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:10.350
That would effectively
erase disqualification

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:11.540
from the Constitution.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:14.100
- So, assuming that this
impeachment trial is legitimate,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:16.540
which the overwhelming
weight of legal authority

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:17.920
seems to suggest,

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.620
that takes us to the arguments

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.880
for the conviction on impeachment

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:23.470
and the arguments against.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.750
On days two and three of
the impeachment trial,

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:28.920
House managers made their
case arguing for conviction.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:30.610
House managers spent most of day two

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:32.750
presenting new footage
of the riots themselves.

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.570
And, honestly, it's shocking.

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:36.230
And let's take a moment to recognize

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:38.580
a true American hero, Eugene Goodman,

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:40.060
who appears to have single-handedly

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:41.370
saved the life of Mitt Romney

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:43.950
and countless other
representatives and senators.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:46.160
A man whose quick thinking saved lives

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:48.140
and whose decision not to use his gun

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.850
also spared the lives
of countless rioters.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.980
And we forget that this
easily could have been

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:54.750
a massacre in two different directions.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.240
Not only the politicians,

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:59.250
but also the rioters and
protestors themselves.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:01.270
And while many would have cheered that on,

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:03.510
that too would have been
an American tragedy.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:05.320
And it's only through the quick thinking

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.070
of people like Eugene Goodman

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:09.440
that that tragedy was averted.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:12.350
So let's start with the
arguments of the House managers.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:13.840
In their trial brief they argue

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.147
that "this trial arises from
President Donald J. Trump's

00:14:16.147 --> 00:14:17.757
"incitement of insurrection

00:14:17.757 --> 00:14:20.400
"against the Republic
he swore to protect."

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:21.720
The House impeachment managers

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:23.500
walk through the lead-up to January 6th,

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:24.940
discussing how the Trump change campaign

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:28.480
refused to conceded that
Trump lost the election.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.740
Even after Trump lost over 60 legal cases

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:31.710
related to the election,

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:33.480
he refused to accept the loss

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.590
and instead saying he won in a landslide

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:37.997
and the vote was stolen from him.

00:14:37.997 --> 00:14:40.030
Former President Trump also pressured

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:41.850
state election officials,
state representatives,

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:43.310
governors and US attorneys

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.440
to overturn the will of
the people in their states.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:47.930
After the Electoral College voted

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:50.170
to certify President Biden's win,

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:51.750
Former President Trump
asked his supporters

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:55.230
to come to Washington on
January 6th to "stop the steal."

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.750
January 6th was the day
that Congress would meet

00:14:57.750 --> 00:14:58.950
to certify the election,

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:00.283
and Trump told his supporters

00:15:00.283 --> 00:15:03.570
that the results could
be changed on that day.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.880
Trump spent approximately $50 million

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.890
on a campaign to stop the steal.

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:10.250
- These ads were designed to run

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:12.373
all the way up to January 5.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:15.170
And then they stopped.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:16.580
- Leading many to suspect

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.700
that the Trump campaign
didn't actually think

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:20.030
that there was any value

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:22.590
in making the arguments after the 6th

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:24.110
and putting everything into that date.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:27.670
The Trump campaign helped
plan the event on January 6th.

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:29.430
Trump selected the date
because this was the day

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:31.280
that Biden's win would be finalized.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:34.020
- The Constitution says you
have to protect our country

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.100
and you have to protect our Constitution

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:38.000
and you can't vote on fraud.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.290
And fraud breaks up
everything, doesn't it?

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:42.260
When you catch somebody in a fraud,

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:45.713
you're allowed to go by
very different rules.

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:50.487
So I hope Mike has the courage
to do what he has to do.

00:15:50.487 --> 00:15:52.585
- He's talking about you!

00:15:52.585 --> 00:15:55.418
(people shouting)

00:15:58.427 --> 00:16:00.120
- And we fight.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:01.350
We fight like hell.

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.020
And if you don't fight like hell,

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:04.890
you're not gonna have a country anymore.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:06.370
- Trump told them that Mike Pence

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:08.160
could overturn the election results.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.450
Right after Trump was informed

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:11.540
that Vice President
Pence had been evacuated

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:14.170
due to all of the supporters
invading the Capitol,

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:18.117
minutes later he tweeted to the
mob that Pence was a coward.

00:16:18.117 --> 00:16:19.577
"Mike Pence didn't have the courage

00:16:19.577 --> 00:16:21.337
"to do what should have been done

00:16:21.337 --> 00:16:23.587
"to protect our country
and our Constitution.

00:16:23.587 --> 00:16:26.130
"USA demands the truth."

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:28.930
The mob yelled Trump's
tweet into a bullhorn

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:30.660
while they went into the Capitol building.

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:32.090
The mob searched for Pence,

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:34.010
the mob searched for the votes,

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:37.020
and the mob searched for
other elected representatives

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:40.450
who were going through with
the election certification.

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:41.800
And as demonstrated by his calls

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:43.180
to Senators Tuberville and Lee,

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:45.250
Trump was willing to use the violence

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:46.730
to his effective advantage,

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.460
as leverage to try and
get them to do something

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:51.000
to stop the certification.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:52.900
So this is the factual basis

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:55.210
for the articles of
impeachment from the House.

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:57.370
Now let's talk about the legal basis.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:59.140
The House argues that these actions

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:00.640
were high crimes and misdemeanors.

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.150
I've done many other videos

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:03.700
on the meaning of high
crimes and misdemeanors,

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:06.170
but suffice to say almost
all legal scholars agree

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:08.290
that the term was meant
to include transgressions

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:10.590
that were not necessarily actual crimes

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:12.200
prohibited by federal law

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:15.090
since there wasn't very much
federal law at the time,

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:18.200
and that it was a catchall for things

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:20.710
that are a violation of public trust.

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:22.210
Certainly actual crimes

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.510
could be high crimes and
misdemeanors as well,

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:28.700
but it's not necessary
that a president be removed

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:30.900
because of an actual statutory crime.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.040
And as we've discussed,

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:34.020
the Founders believed that
the actions against the state

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:36.400
which subvert the
government were impeachable,

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.240
as Alexander Hamilton described them

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.107
as actions which are "political,

00:17:40.107 --> 00:17:42.647
"as they relate chiefly to
injuries done immediately

00:17:42.647 --> 00:17:44.350
"to society itself."

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:46.650
Historically, politicians
have taken the position

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:48.680
that these offenses could be even moral,

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:50.840
such as having an affair
and then lying under oath

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:51.740
to cover it up.

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:52.670
The House Democrats write

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:54.707
that "if provoking an insurrection riot

00:17:54.707 --> 00:17:56.347
"against a Joint Session Congress

00:17:56.347 --> 00:17:57.647
"after losing an election

00:17:57.647 --> 00:17:59.157
"is not an impeachable offense,

00:17:59.157 --> 00:18:01.320
"it's hard to image what would be."

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.810
The House argues that Trump
attacks the foundations

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.667
of the democratic process itself.

00:18:05.667 --> 00:18:07.867
"Under absolutely no
circumstance may a candidate

00:18:07.867 --> 00:18:09.837
"for any position at
any level of government

00:18:09.837 --> 00:18:13.620
"respond to electoral defeat
by provoking armed violence."

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:14.670
And the House managers,

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:16.840
anticipating the arguments
of Trump's defense

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:18.530
that he had a First Amendment right

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.750
to say whatever he wanted
whenever he wanted,

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.840
the House managers have
two counterarguments

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:24.500
that the First Amendment is not a defense

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:27.200
to the actions and
statements that were made

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:30.560
on January 6th and the
lead-up to January 6th.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:32.300
First they say that President
Trump should be held

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:34.639
to a higher standard of
conduct than a private citizen.

00:18:34.639 --> 00:18:36.677
"No one would seriously suggest

00:18:36.677 --> 00:18:39.097
"that a president should be
immunized from impeachment

00:18:39.097 --> 00:18:40.827
"if he publicly championed the adoption

00:18:40.827 --> 00:18:42.087
"of a totalitarian government,

00:18:42.087 --> 00:18:45.157
"swore an oath of eternal
loyalty to a foreign power,

00:18:45.157 --> 00:18:47.237
"or advocated that states secede from

00:18:47.237 --> 00:18:48.697
"and overthrow the Union,

00:18:48.697 --> 00:18:50.707
"even though private
citizens could be protected

00:18:50.707 --> 00:18:53.240
"by the First Amendment for such speech."

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:55.340
The House managers argue that interfering

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:57.910
with the peaceful transfer
of power with violence

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:01.080
is an assault on core
First Amendment principles.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:02.050
And the House's second argument

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:03.470
is that there's already quite a bit

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:04.800
of First Amendment jurisprudence

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:06.950
on the limits of some speech.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:08.987
That speech is not protected where it is

00:19:08.987 --> 00:19:11.757
"directed to inciting or
producing imminent lawless action

00:19:11.757 --> 00:19:15.000
"and is likely to incite
or produce such action."

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.300
I've already covered whether
Former President Trump's speech

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:19.060
incited the riot in another video

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:21.020
from a criminal law perspective,

00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:23.650
and there's certainly
arguments on either side,

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:25.180
but the impeachment is not about

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:28.300
whether Former President Trump
violated a criminal statute

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:29.910
and is going to jail.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.550
The impeachment trial
literally cannot result

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:33.930
in a jail conviction.

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.880
It says specifically in the Constitution

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.330
that that is not allowed.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:39.910
It's about whether his conduct
as a whole was so serious

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:42.117
that America should send a
message to future presidents

00:19:42.117 --> 00:19:43.880
and to insurrectionists

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.920
in whether it should punish the speech

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:47.330
that was actually made

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:49.680
by preventing such a transgressor

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:51.780
from being able to hold office again.

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:53.340
And that takes us to the third argument

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:55.020
of the Democratic House managers

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:58.760
which is, again, anticipating
arguments from the Trump camp.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:00.310
They argue that late impeachments

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:02.440
are absolutely constitutional.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:03.700
The House cites the same authorities

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:05.090
that we talked about earlier in this video

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:06.580
in talking about how late impeachments

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:09.490
are both constitutional and
have a fairly long history,

00:20:09.490 --> 00:20:10.840
and they also rely on old writings

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.120
of Professor Jonathan Turley,

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:14.090
one of Trump's chief defenders.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:15.720
Turley wrote several journal articles

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:18.620
that impeaching ex-presidents
is constitutional.

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:19.880
- [Man] Professor Turley argued

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:21.180
that the House could have impeached

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:23.620
and the Senate could
have tried Richard Nixon

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:25.067
after he resigned.

00:20:25.067 --> 00:20:27.277
"Future presidents could not assume

00:20:27.277 --> 00:20:31.027
"that mere resignation would
avoid a trial of their conduct

00:20:31.027 --> 00:20:32.117
"in the United States Senate.

00:20:32.117 --> 00:20:36.067
"No man in no circumstance
can escape the account

00:20:36.067 --> 00:20:39.110
"which he owes to the
laws of his country."

00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:40.760
- Of course, it's also
important to point out

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:43.260
that President Trump was not
impeached after he left office.

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:45.840
He was impeached when he was in office

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:47.930
and now it's up to the Senate to try.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.060
And, in fact, as many have pointed out,

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:52.130
Mitch McConnell could
have allowed the Senate

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:53.710
to conduct the trial

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:56.550
before Former President Trump left office.

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.980
But this is not a case of
the impeachment happening

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.700
after the resignation or after
the official has left office,

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:05.270
the impeachment, the indictment
phase already happened

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:07.100
while Trump was president.

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:10.190
Of course, Turley has been
featured heavily on Fox News

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:12.850
arguing the exact opposite
of his earlier views

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:14.240
without saying why and how

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:17.060
his hundreds of pages of
arguments are now false.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:19.860
But Jonathan Turley is
going to Jonathan Turley.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:23.440
- What they're citing is an
article from 21 years ago.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:24.277
- And the House managers' arguments

00:21:24.277 --> 00:21:26.930
are also sound in classic
penological theory,

00:21:26.930 --> 00:21:27.763
one point arguing-

00:21:27.763 --> 00:21:31.270
- It's making sure that
no future official,

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:35.240
no future president does
the same exact thing

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:36.600
President Trump does.

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:39.010
- What they're arguing
is in fact deterrence,

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:40.170
both individual and general.

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:42.970
They want to make sure that
Former President Trump himself

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:45.650
never has an opportunity to
do the things that he did,

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:46.880
as well as making sure that no one

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:48.350
like Former President Trump

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:49.810
does it in the future as well,

00:21:49.810 --> 00:21:53.290
as well as the common
justification of punitive measures,

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:54.870
punishing Former President Trump

00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:56.900
for the actions that he did undertake.

00:21:56.900 --> 00:21:57.880
Those are the classic reasons

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:00.320
as to why you would
incarcerate someone in jail

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:01.430
in the first place.

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:05.000
So that takes us to Former
President Trump's arguments.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:07.130
The House impeachment
managers cite Trump's actions

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:08.190
and words and conduct

00:22:08.190 --> 00:22:10.550
from before, during, and after the riot.

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:11.480
Trump, on the other hand,

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:14.090
has chosen to focus on narrow issues

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:17.420
of whether his January 6th
speech would have made him liable

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:18.430
for the actual crime

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:21.210
of incitement of imminent lawless action.

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:23.210
Trump camp argues that President Trump

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:25.480
did not commit the crime of incitement

00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:29.320
and focus on the text of
the January 6th speech.

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:30.857
Trump argued in his brief,

00:22:30.857 --> 00:22:31.690
"Words do matter

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:34.137
"and the words of President
Trump's January 6th speech

00:22:34.137 --> 00:22:35.397
"speak for themselves.

00:22:35.397 --> 00:22:37.017
"Of the over 10,000 words spoken,

00:22:37.017 --> 00:22:38.297
"Mr. Trump used the word fight

00:22:38.297 --> 00:22:39.727
"a little more than a handful of times

00:22:39.727 --> 00:22:41.517
"and each time in a figurative sense

00:22:41.517 --> 00:22:43.937
"that has long been
accepted in public discourse

00:22:43.937 --> 00:22:46.557
"when urging people to
stand and use their voices

00:22:46.557 --> 00:22:48.450
"to be heard on matters important."

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:49.850
As the Trump camp points out,

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:51.930
the former president
spoke for about an hour

00:22:51.930 --> 00:22:53.470
but never advocated for violence

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:55.750
or told anyone to get their weapons.

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:57.540
Although he told them to
head over to the Capitol,

00:22:57.540 --> 00:22:58.560
he did not go with them

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:00.540
nor did he tell them
to storm the building.

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:02.030
And the Trump camp is focused on language

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:04.290
that doesn't fit the
House managers' narrative,

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.077
focusing on statements like,

00:23:06.077 --> 00:23:08.017
"I know that everyone
here will soon be marching

00:23:08.017 --> 00:23:09.037
"over to the Capitol building

00:23:09.037 --> 00:23:12.310
"to peacefully and patriotically
make your voices heard."

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:13.970
And the next big argument
that the Trump camp makes

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:16.200
is that the Senate simply
doesn't have jurisdiction

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.990
over a former public official,

00:23:17.990 --> 00:23:20.090
citing the constitutional
text we referred to earlier.

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:22.480
Former President Trump's
lawyers spent most of day four

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:24.310
laying out their defense as it was.

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:26.210
And by the way, I'm not
particularly impressed

00:23:26.210 --> 00:23:28.240
by Trump's legal defense team here.

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:30.790
With respect to Bruce Castor,

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:32.630
he legitimately seemed to be confused

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:33.810
at many different times.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:35.850
It seems like he winged everything,

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:37.240
and often when it was clear

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:38.910
he was not talking from his notes,

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:40.020
he would go back to his notes,

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:42.910
and sometimes things that he
had written out ahead of time

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:45.160
were worse than the things
he was just talking about

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:46.020
off the cuff.

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:47.600
- You know, it's interesting because

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:50.230
I don't wanna steal the
thunder from the other lawyers,

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:52.930
but Nebraska you're going to hear

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:57.580
is quite a judicial thinking place.

00:23:57.580 --> 00:23:58.730
- With respect to David Schoen,

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:00.210
he seemed to think that
the louder he yelled

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:01.590
the more persuasive he was,

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.460
and he was very wrong about that.

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:04.810
And then there's Michael Van Der Veen

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:06.410
who is a personal injury lawyer

00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:08.400
who absolutely looked and sounded

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:10.290
like a personal injury lawyer

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:12.960
who had absolutely no understanding

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:15.260
of the grand stakes that he was on.

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:18.130
- These depositions
should be done in person,

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:20.720
in my office, in Philadelphia.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:22.430
- This was a B team of lawyers

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:24.210
filling in for Former President Trump

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:26.540
after his first legal team quit en mass,

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:27.830
and it absolutely showed.

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:29.500
And many times the Trump team argued

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:32.460
that Former President Trump
was not afforded due process

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:35.530
and therefore the whole thing
should just be thrown out.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:38.070
This argument I find particularly galling

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:39.400
because on the one hand they're saying

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:41.210
that investigations should be done,

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:42.337
depositions should be taken,

00:24:42.337 --> 00:24:46.190
and at the exact same
time they're also arguing

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:48.530
that the whole thing should be thrown out

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:50.230
because too much time was taken,

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:51.240
too much time has passed,

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:53.670
and now Trump is no longer in office

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:55.230
and thus there's no jurisdiction

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:56.640
for this impeachment at all.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:59.330
And yes, lawyers argue in
the alternative all the time,

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:00.163
but that doesn't mean

00:25:00.163 --> 00:25:02.710
that that's a persuasive tactic to take.

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:06.000
And here, as we've
talked about many times,

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.960
the requirements for a criminal conviction

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.250
simply don't apply here.

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:11.980
The beyond reasonably doubt standard

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:14.040
does not apply in impeachments.

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:15.780
And there's a lot of confusion

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:18.330
about the term incitement itself.

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:19.830
Incitement means a lot
of different things.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:22.950
It is, number one, a crime
under most jurisdictions.

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:25.390
It's also an exception
to the First Amendment

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:27.610
in terms of speech that is not protected

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:28.750
by the First Amendment.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:30.737
It is a political offense

00:25:30.737 --> 00:25:33.380
as to something that the Senate might feel

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:35.730
is unbecoming of the president himself

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:37.590
and thus warrants removal

00:25:37.590 --> 00:25:39.780
or being barred from public office.

00:25:39.780 --> 00:25:42.300
And it's also being
used as just a shorthand

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:44.170
for everything that happened.

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:46.550
And because you have these
different definitions

00:25:46.550 --> 00:25:48.260
going across purposes,

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:49.490
a lot of the different arguments

00:25:49.490 --> 00:25:51.310
are like ships passing in the night.

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:52.790
And it's all bound up in this question

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:54.960
of whether the president should
be held to a higher standard

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:55.910
than your average citizen

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:57.210
or a lower one.

00:25:57.210 --> 00:25:58.990
And remember, the vast
majority of legal weight

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:00.670
says that the president
doesn't have to be guilty

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:02.210
of an actual crime

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:05.330
to be impeached, removed,
and barred from office.

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:07.100
And playing off of that confusion,

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:09.920
the Trump lawyers created a video montage

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:13.640
of Democrats using the
term fight in context

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:16.350
that don't rise to the
level of incitement.

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:18.740
Of course, many people argued the counter

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:21.740
that of course it depends on the context,

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:23.530
that it also depends on the intent.

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:25.830
None of these Democratic
members of Congress

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:28.910
ever had the intention to
actually incite violence,

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:31.330
that the violence was not incited,

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:33.170
no riot ever came about of these words,

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:35.200
and that the context was
incredibly different.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:37.010
But the point is valid
that different words

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:39.770
can mean different things
in different contexts,

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:41.360
but it really feels like these arguments

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:43.940
were a false equivalence
and a lot of what aboutism.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:45.620
And apart from the due process arguments,

00:26:45.620 --> 00:26:49.430
the Trump lawyers argued
extensively about hearsay,

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:52.010
and this makes me want
to pull out my own hair

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:53.280
for several different reasons.

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:54.960
First of all, as we've talked
about many different times,

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.670
the Senate can use
whatever standard it wants,

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:59.780
and generally it has almost
always allowed hearsay

00:26:59.780 --> 00:27:02.160
as evidence in impeachment trials.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:02.993
But more to the point,

00:27:02.993 --> 00:27:05.680
almost all of the hearsay
statements at issue here

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:07.410
would be admissible in trial

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:08.820
because they weren't being used

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:10.150
for the truth of the matter asserted,

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:11.710
which is necessary for hearsay.

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:13.750
But rather, they were being used

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:16.960
for the state of mind of
Former President Trump

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:18.120
and his intent,

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:21.080
which is a non-hearsay purpose
to the statements themselves.

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:22.870
So yes, they were out-of-court statements,

00:27:22.870 --> 00:27:25.580
but not all out-of-court
statements are inadmissible,

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:28.350
and the vast majority of the
things being discussed here

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:30.380
would probably have been admissible.

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:32.880
And putting apart the fact
that almost all of the senators

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:35.580
who are participating
in this particular trial

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:36.960
were firsthand witnesses

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:39.600
and the hearsay wasn't really necessary

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:40.960
for them to make their decisions

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:43.100
because they were a part in person

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:44.860
to witnessing all of this stuff.

00:27:44.860 --> 00:27:47.350
But perhaps the biggest
irony is that the defense

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:50.370
extensively used hearsay themselves.

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:51.570
The entire defense case,

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:55.070
because President Trump wasn't
willing to testify himself,

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:57.140
relied on out-of-court statements.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:58.450
So if you're going to exclude

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:01.210
a whole bunch of the prosecution's case

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:03.140
on the basis they're
out-of-court statements,

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:05.780
then you have to disregard
the entire defense case.

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:07.470
Now there was big news
after the fourth day

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:08.480
of the impeachment trial

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:10.730
when Representative Herrera
Beutler, a Republican,

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:12.690
came forward and relayed

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:14.350
part of the conversation that she had

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:16.800
with Representative Kevin
McCarthy of California.

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:18.880
This conversation seemed to dispute

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:21.220
basically everything the defense had said

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:23.510
about Former President Trump's knowledge

00:28:23.510 --> 00:28:25.390
about what was going on at the time.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:27.383
- In the middle of the insurrection,

00:28:28.220 --> 00:28:31.080
when House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:33.370
called the President to beg for help,

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:35.847
President Trump responded, and I quote,

00:28:35.847 --> 00:28:38.247
"Well, Kevin, I guess these people

00:28:38.247 --> 00:28:41.490
"are more upset about the
election than you are."

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:42.450
- Now if this were a court case

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:44.150
it is possible to get double hearsay

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:45.980
or hearsay within
hearsay to be admissible,

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:48.600
you just need an exception for
both layers of the hearsay.

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:49.890
And arguably that's met,

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:52.170
because between Trump and McCarthy

00:28:52.170 --> 00:28:54.350
that would go to President
Trump's state of mind,

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:56.600
and as between McCarthy
and Herrera Beutler

00:28:56.600 --> 00:28:58.660
that could be a present sense impression

00:28:58.660 --> 00:29:00.080
or excited utterance.

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:01.603
Based on the circumstances,

00:29:03.018 --> 00:29:04.870
there would be an exception
to the hearsay rule

00:29:04.870 --> 00:29:07.970
and arguably that statement
would be admissible in court.

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:09.420
And that takes us to the third argument

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:10.253
that the Trump lawyers make,

00:29:10.253 --> 00:29:12.510
which is that an impeachment
of a private citizen

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:14.087
is really a bill of attainder.

00:29:14.087 --> 00:29:15.730
Now this is an argument that probably made

00:29:15.730 --> 00:29:18.340
a lot of first-year law
students extremely happy,

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:21.620
because bills of attainder
don't come up that often.

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:23.780
But Trump argues that the
impeachment trial by the Senate

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:25.090
of a private citizen

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:27.540
violates Article I, Section
9 of the US Constitution

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:30.610
which states that "no bill of
attainder shall be passed."

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:31.840
What is a bill of attainder?

00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:34.390
Well, that is basically
a prohibition on Congress

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:37.630
from enacting any law that
legislatively determines guilt

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:40.920
and inflicts punishment upon
an identifiable individual

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:44.780
without provisions of the
protections of a judicial trial.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:45.950
Now according to Trump,

00:29:45.950 --> 00:29:47.660
this impeachment and every impeachment

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:48.670
is a bill of attainder

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:50.060
because it allows the legislature

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:52.600
to make a finding of guilt.

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:54.480
A bill of attainder is the
kind of legislative act

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:56.080
that only has effect when it's adopted

00:29:56.080 --> 00:29:57.250
by both houses of Congress

00:29:57.250 --> 00:29:59.430
and signed into law by the president.

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:01.710
Impeachment and removal,
by contrast, though,

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:04.290
can be accomplished by Congress alone,

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:06.270
and most importantly it's written

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:08.410
into the text of the Constitution.

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:11.220
So if a bill of attainder were to nullify

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:12.870
the impeachment clause,

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:14.210
that would be a big problem,

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:16.780
and there's no interpretation
of the Constitution

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:19.320
where that can possibly be correct.

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:22.200
And this means that Trump is
legally incorrect in saying

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.870
that a conviction of the Senate

00:30:23.870 --> 00:30:25.690
counts as a bill of attainder.

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:27.640
The Senate isn't voting on any such bill,

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:30.710
it's putting Former
President Trump on trial

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:34.620
for impeachment, which is part
of the Constitution itself.

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:37.510
The President Joe Biden doesn't
have a role in impeachment.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:39.410
And there've been all kind
of bad faith arguments

00:30:39.410 --> 00:30:41.580
about saying that you
can't deprive someone

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:44.360
of life liberty or property
without due process,

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:47.880
but the due process is the
impeachment trial itself.

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:49.210
And on top of that,

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:50.820
this is not a depravation

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:52.760
of the normal Fourth Amendment rights.

00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:54.420
It's written into the Constitution

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:56.940
that you can have an impeachment

00:30:56.940 --> 00:30:59.610
that has the punishment
of preventing someone

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:01.140
from being able to hold office again.

00:31:01.140 --> 00:31:03.580
It's written into the Constitution itself.

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:04.590
But this distinction

00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:06.410
between a bill of attainder
and an impeachment

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:08.880
goes back far into English common law.

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:11.420
Under the law, English
monarchs would have to sign off

00:31:11.420 --> 00:31:12.880
on any bills of attainder.

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:14.610
And impeachment was added as a tool

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:17.040
precisely because it did
not need royal consent,

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:18.890
it gave Parliament the
ability to constrain

00:31:18.890 --> 00:31:20.030
the executive power.

00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:21.450
And the American Founders were not fond

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:22.370
of bills of attainder

00:31:22.370 --> 00:31:24.150
because they effectively
allowed the government

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:26.020
to a make a post-hoc decision

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:28.020
about punishing someone criminally

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.180
as an individual private citizen.

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:31.870
But they really liked impeachment

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:34.030
because they feared a monarch

00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:35.470
coming to the American executive.

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:38.180
This was the whole reason for
the Revolution to begin with,

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:40.430
that they wanted to
cast aside the monarchy.

00:31:40.430 --> 00:31:42.320
So this takes us to
one of the main thrusts

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:43.500
of the Trump lawyers,

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:45.250
which is that the First
Amendment prohibits

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:47.340
impeachment for words.

00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:49.420
Trump argues that the
impeachment invites senators

00:31:49.420 --> 00:31:50.880
to violate the First Amendment principle

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:51.850
of freedom of speech.

00:31:51.850 --> 00:31:53.520
And Trump says that impeachment itself

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:55.577
is always constrained by free speech.

00:31:55.577 --> 00:31:56.937
"The Constitution must, at a minimum,

00:31:56.937 --> 00:31:59.267
"serve as a limitation on
the ability of Congress

00:31:59.267 --> 00:32:01.200
"to impeach for high
crimes and misdemeanors."

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.700
Here, Trump is essentially
saying that the government,

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:05.070
i.e. the House of Representatives,

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:06.970
is trampling on his free speech rights

00:32:06.970 --> 00:32:09.920
by impeaching him for
things that he said and did.

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:10.940
And the crux of this argument

00:32:10.940 --> 00:32:12.630
is that Congress cannot impeach a person

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:14.450
for constitutional conduct.

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:16.640
And if we tried to apply
this principle consistently,

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:18.840
then other articles of impeachment
against other presidents

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:20.580
would also have to be rejected.

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:21.700
Bill Clinton arguably had

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:23.610
a Fourth and Fourteenth
Amendment privacy right

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:25.750
to consensual sexual
conduct with another adult

00:32:25.750 --> 00:32:27.327
and a First Amendment
right to lie about it.

00:32:27.327 --> 00:32:29.960
But the problem is there's
all kinds of legally

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:31.620
and morally impermissible acts

00:32:31.620 --> 00:32:33.500
that are based solely in speech.

00:32:33.500 --> 00:32:36.450
Defamation is a solely speech-based act.

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:37.283
For the most part,

00:32:37.283 --> 00:32:40.100
bribery tends to be a speech-based act.

00:32:40.100 --> 00:32:43.429
When you're making a bargain
with an elected official

00:32:43.429 --> 00:32:45.430
for their own pecuniary interests,

00:32:45.430 --> 00:32:47.380
that's based in speech.

00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:50.320
And there's basically no argument
that some of these things

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:52.080
wouldn't give rise to impeachment.

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:53.850
And the Trump lawyers are trying to blend

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:56.070
legal and political arguments together.

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:57.760
The Trump lawyers accused
the House managers

00:32:57.760 --> 00:32:59.130
of creating an impeachment process

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:02.160
that yields to the "unbridled
whims of House managers."

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:04.700
But a bipartisan group of
legislators in the House

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:07.990
believed that Trump's conduct
and words were impeachable.

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:09.650
Trump argues that they were all motivated

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:11.620
by Trump derangement syndrome,

00:33:11.620 --> 00:33:13.000
but this isn't really a legal argument,

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:13.910
it's a political one,

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:16.090
and it's one that of
course he's free to make

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:19.790
but it doesn't seem to hold
water on a legal basis.

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:20.690
The House of course argues

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:21.890
that it didn't impeach President Trump

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:24.030
because he expressed an
unpopular political opinion,

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:25.210
but rather it impeached him

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:27.810
because he was willfully
inciting a violent insurrection

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:29.000
against the government.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.597
They argued, "We live in a nation

00:33:30.597 --> 00:33:31.647
"governed by the rule of law,

00:33:31.647 --> 00:33:33.547
"not mob violence incited by presidents

00:33:33.547 --> 00:33:36.330
"who cannot accept their
own electoral defeat."

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:37.710
And of course President Trump argues

00:33:37.710 --> 00:33:40.150
that he did not commit a violation of law.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:42.710
Trump argues the same
position in this impeachment

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:43.830
that he did the first time around,

00:33:43.830 --> 00:33:45.200
that a president can only be impeached

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:46.940
if he violates a criminal statute.

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:49.080
The Trump lawyers argue that
the only relevant inquiry

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:50.820
is whether Trump's January 6th speech

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:52.740
incited people to imminent lawless action,

00:33:52.740 --> 00:33:54.410
and to determine whether it did

00:33:54.410 --> 00:33:56.520
we should not consider
the broader environment

00:33:56.520 --> 00:33:58.310
in which this speech was actually made,

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:02.090
we should only look narrowly
at the actual text itself.

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:03.760
And on the fifth day of
the impeachment trial,

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:05.300
the big drama came as a result

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:06.660
of the Democrats winning a vote

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:08.990
to be able to call witnesses at the trial

00:34:08.990 --> 00:34:09.930
but then backing down,

00:34:09.930 --> 00:34:13.130
accepting an stipulation of
the Herrera Beutler statement,

00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:15.210
and then moving forward
with closing arguments.

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:18.400
Now, personally, I would have
liked to have seen witnesses.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:20.800
I think that it would have been
good for historical reasons

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:23.420
to lay even more of a factual foundation

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:24.560
for everything that went on

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:27.850
and to be able to force
some of the testimony

00:34:27.850 --> 00:34:31.420
related to things that
Former President Trump

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:33.090
may or may not have been thinking

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:34.780
and when he was thinking them.

00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:36.740
But at the same time it's
probably not the case

00:34:36.740 --> 00:34:39.040
that witnesses would have
actually changed the result

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:40.530
of this impeachment trial.

00:34:40.530 --> 00:34:41.363
And at the end of the day

00:34:41.363 --> 00:34:44.160
57 senators voted to convict
Former President Trump,

00:34:44.160 --> 00:34:46.320
which was not enough for the 2/3 majority

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:47.650
needed for conviction.

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:49.140
And the number is a record.

00:34:49.140 --> 00:34:50.770
There were seven Republican senators

00:34:50.770 --> 00:34:52.270
who voted to convict Trump,

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:53.810
and that is seven times greater

00:34:53.810 --> 00:34:56.310
than the previous number of senators

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:59.330
who voted to convict a
president of their own party,

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:02.840
which that number was set
by Senator Mitt Romney

00:35:02.840 --> 00:35:04.530
just a year ago in President Trump's

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:06.270
first impeachment trial.

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:09.470
So the question is, what happens next?

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:11.720
I suspect that there will
probably be commissions,

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:15.580
subpoenas, investigations,
and criminal prosecutions.

00:35:15.580 --> 00:35:18.100
Remember, the impeachment
punishment is extremely narrow.

00:35:18.100 --> 00:35:20.290
It's simply removal from office

00:35:20.290 --> 00:35:22.360
and being barred from running again.

00:35:22.360 --> 00:35:24.980
Theoretically, impeachment itself is dead.

00:35:24.980 --> 00:35:26.960
If under these circumstances

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:28.690
the president cannot be impeached,

00:35:28.690 --> 00:35:32.140
then it doesn't seem like
there is any realistic scenario

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:34.120
where any president
will ever be impeached,

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:36.830
which in itself is I
think a worrying trend.

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:38.020
And even though Mitch McConnell voted

00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:39.530
to acquit President Trump,

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:41.850
he still recognizes that Trump is liable

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:43.240
for everything that he did in office

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:46.370
and seems to be almost asking prosecutors

00:35:46.370 --> 00:35:49.000
to criminally prosecute
Former President Trump.

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:54.000
- They did this because they'd
been fed wild falsehoods

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:57.530
by the most powerful man on Earth.

00:35:57.530 --> 00:35:58.390
- And bad news for those

00:35:58.390 --> 00:36:00.080
that want to invoke the
Fourteenth Amendment

00:36:00.080 --> 00:36:01.910
to bar people who have participated

00:36:01.910 --> 00:36:03.180
in sedition against the United States

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:04.470
from holding office.

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:08.210
The 57 Senate votes that were secured

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:10.410
to convict President Trump on impeachment

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:12.220
are not enough votes for cloture,

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:15.290
so given that the filibuster still exists,

00:36:15.290 --> 00:36:16.730
there's not going to be enough votes

00:36:16.730 --> 00:36:19.630
to actually bar him under
the Fourteenth Amendment,

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:21.660
assuming that the votes stay the same.

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:22.690
But there is some good news.

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:25.980
Given that this was
truly a bipartisan effort

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:28.680
with both members of the House
and seven Republican senators

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:30.500
who voted for conviction,

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.970
this is probably a green light
for criminal prosecutions.

00:36:33.970 --> 00:36:37.010
Normally you would assume
that state district attorneys

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:38.720
or the US Attorney General

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:40.770
would be extremely reluctant

00:36:40.770 --> 00:36:44.050
to engage in a criminal
investigation of a former president.

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:46.830
But given the overwhelming
bipartisan support,

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:49.590
it sort of signals to those
law enforcement officers

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:52.690
that you can proceed if there is indeed

00:36:52.690 --> 00:36:55.840
a true criminal violation
by Former President Trump.

00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:57.300
And there's also more good news

00:36:57.300 --> 00:36:59.050
in that the threat of impeachment

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:01.300
probably restrained Former President Trump

00:37:01.300 --> 00:37:03.500
in the last days of his presidency.

00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:04.910
So generally I'm feeling pretty good

00:37:04.910 --> 00:37:06.050
about this whole affair.

00:37:06.050 --> 00:37:08.130
Yes, I wish they had called witnesses

00:37:08.130 --> 00:37:10.140
and made the record even more clear

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:12.750
and, frankly, rubbed the
noses of the Republicans

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:14.920
in their own filth for
a little bit longer,

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:17.480
but the record was already 1,000% clear.

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:19.540
This was not a failing
by the House managers

00:37:19.540 --> 00:37:20.630
to make a case.

00:37:20.630 --> 00:37:23.440
The outcome was already a fait accompli.

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:25.070
Mitch McConnell wouldn't allow trial

00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:26.437
while Trump was still in office

00:37:26.437 --> 00:37:28.360
and then hid behind his
own political maneuvering

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:31.040
as a reason for why he
couldn't vote for conviction.

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:31.873
And let's face it,

00:37:31.873 --> 00:37:35.170
sometimes even futile
gestures are worth doing

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:37.210
because it's the right thing to do.

00:37:37.210 --> 00:37:39.270
Seven Republicans voted for conviction

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:41.350
and that's absolutely no small feat

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:43.480
in this hyper-partisan world.

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:45.970
This is not a win for the Trump camp.

00:37:45.970 --> 00:37:47.730
They should not be high fiving,

00:37:47.730 --> 00:37:49.940
thinking that they did a good job here.

00:37:49.940 --> 00:37:52.680
And I think history will be
absolutely clear on this point.

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:54.240
It will show that the acquittal

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:55.800
was a miscarriage of justice,

00:37:55.800 --> 00:37:57.910
just like the acquittal of Andrew Johnson

00:37:57.910 --> 00:38:02.010
and the acquittal of Donald
Trump the first time around.

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:03.870
But let me know if you
agree with my analysis,

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:05.330
leave your objections in the comments,

00:38:05.330 --> 00:38:06.450
and check out this playlist over here

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:07.980
where I talk about all of the legal issues

00:38:07.980 --> 00:38:09.280
that happened in the Trump administration

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:10.900
that led up to the impeachment.

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:14.083
So click on this playlist,
or I'll see you in court.

